10. Parallelism
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

GRIDLESS cutoff/direct-access work uses the same two-level scheduling concept as
Mode3D: MPI distributes coarse chunks between processes and a shared-memory backend
processes independent trajectory tasks concurrently inside each rank.

Configuration
-------------

Input-file controls:

  GRIDLESS_PARALLEL             THREADS
  GRIDLESS_THREADS              16
  GRIDLESS_MPI_SCHEDULER        DYNAMIC
  GRIDLESS_MPI_DYNAMIC_CHUNK    0

Equivalent command-line controls:

  -gridless-parallel THREADS
  -gridless-threads 16
  -gridless-mpi-scheduler DYNAMIC
  -gridless-mpi-dynamic-chunk N     # optional; omit/0 for automatic

`THREADS` is the recommended backend for the standalone direct-field cutoff path.
`OPENMP` and `SERIAL` remain supported through the common backend resolver.  The thread
count is per MPI rank, so `-np 4 -gridless-threads 16` requests up to 64 trajectory
workers across a node, subject to CPU affinity and available cores.

Dynamic MPI + local threads
---------------------------

The rank/main thread is the only thread that calls MPI.  In DYNAMIC mode it atomically
fetches a chunk of flattened trajectory task ids, hands those tasks to the local worker
team, waits for the team to finish, accumulates the results, and then updates the MPI
completion counter.  Worker threads never enter MPI, so MPI_THREAD_MULTIPLE is not
required.

Each worker owns a private `cFieldEvaluator`.  Evaluators are constructed serially on
the rank/main thread before workers start, avoiding concurrent Geopack/model-library
initialization.  Worker results are returned through private `ResultMsg` slots and are
merged by the rank/main thread after join; shared cutoff/output arrays therefore need no
worker-side locking.

Automatic dynamic chunking
--------------------------

`GRIDLESS_MPI_DYNAMIC_CHUNK <= 0` means automatic.  The shared scheduler receives the
actual GRIDLESS worker count and currently chooses a chunk of approximately four work
items per local worker, limited by the global task count.  This is deliberate: the old
serial GRIDLESS code effectively used a one-task fetch, which would starve a 16-thread
rank.  If an explicit chunk is used, it should normally be at least the worker count.

C19 adaptive `DIRECT_ACCESS` is a special case because one top-level work item is an
entire sky direction containing many sequential seed/refinement trajectories.  The C19
runner therefore writes a worker-sized chunk by default (for example 16 tasks for 16
workers) instead of leaving the generic four-items-per-worker AUTO heuristic in effect.
This reduces MPI-rank straggler tails while still giving every local worker one adaptive
direction.  Explicit user chunk settings continue to override the runner heuristic.

Frozen-field thread-safety guard
--------------------------------

Standalone Tsyganenko/Geopack field parameters are process-global.  Concurrent direct
field evaluation is therefore enabled only when all locations in one process use the
same frozen epoch/driver snapshot.  This includes POINTS, SHELLS, and C19's normal
one-epoch-per-process runs.  If a TRAJECTORY input contains multiple UTC epochs, GRIDLESS
prints a warning and falls back to SERIAL intra-rank execution while retaining MPI
parallelism.  This avoids racing a process-global Geopack state between epochs.

The live SWMF-coupled gridless field sampler is also kept out of this standalone worker
pool because it uses AMPS coupler-mesh interpolation with a different thread-safety
contract.

Performance
-----------

The runtime benefit depends on field model, mover, geometry, and the fraction of long
near-cutoff trajectories.  For C19, combine GRIDLESS threading with
`VECTOR_APERTURES`: pruning the full sphere reduces the task count, while the worker
pool parallelizes the remaining cutoff and direct `A(E,Omega)` trajectories inside each
MPI rank.

Adaptive C19 DIRECT_ACCESS
--------------------------
GRIDLESS implements the same adaptive direct-access sampler as Mode3D through the shared
`util/AdaptiveDirectAccess.h` helper.  When
`CUTOFF_DIRECT_ACCESS_ADAPTIVE T` is used with `CUTOFF_SEARCH_ALGORITHM DIRECT_ACCESS`,
`CUTOFF_RIGIDITY_LIST_GV` is interpreted as a mandatory coarse seed list rather than as
a dense final grid.  One top-level GRIDLESS scheduler task owns one selected sky
direction.  It evaluates every seed, performs the configured forced geometric-midpoint
guard probes, and then recursively bisects intervals whose endpoint states differ.  The
default C19 settings are max depth 6 and guard depth 1.  The three trajectory states are
unchanged: PHYSICAL_FORBIDDEN, ALLOWED, and UNRESOLVED.  No monotonic cutoff is assumed.

The full dyadic/geometric candidate tree is deterministic on every MPI rank.  Evaluated
nodes are written into a direction-private slice of the fixed state array; unused nodes
remain at the -1 sentinel.  This preserves the existing sentinel + MPI_MAX reduction
without requiring every direction to evaluate the same rigidity nodes.  The output
writer omits unused -1 nodes, so the resulting
`cutoff_gridless_dir_access_point_####.dat` can have a different internal energy grid
for each direction while retaining common seed/support nodes and the same public schema
as Mode3D.  Dense behavior is available by setting
`CUTOFF_DIRECT_ACCESS_ADAPTIVE F`; in dense mode an uncomputed -1 state remains a fatal
consistency error.

Guard probes are important because equal coarse endpoint states do not prove that no
narrow access pocket exists between them.  After the guard depth, only visibly changing
endpoint states are refined.  An UNRESOLVED/UNRESOLVED interval is not expanded through
the full tree after its guard probes, since rigidity subdivision cannot repair a trace
time/step/path limit.  C19 post-processing retains such intervals as explicit uncertainty.
See `test/C19/README.md` for the complete algorithm, detector-response fold, convergence
controls, and dense-reference procedure.

